Z||000008||bHooper||02-20-2001||09:25 AM||||dude, that's enough to make me want to give up using heater's in the garage!<P>i think i will just start layering cloth's now.<P>i am so sorry!<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT<BR>KYB's, Eibach's,<BR>Poly Bushings||||reg||1
Z||000009||151 in. of love||02-20-2001||09:33 AM||||  The Fire Department is volunteer and is not liable for anything they do. <BR>  I already talked to my lawyer to see what he thought.<P>   I have never seen a fire go so fast. (I have been in 2 house fires when I was a kid) As soon as they hit it with water, the whole place  went up in a huge fire ball, just engulfing everything.<BR>    They had parked the trucks too close to the garage and after they had gotten there. They had to RE-PARK! the trucks because they made the fire so bad. 2-hoses spraying directly on the part of the fire THEY started. I couldn't believe my eyes. (it is a town of 4000 people, so trying to get them to admit or go against one another would be impossible). <BR>   Can I really blame anyone but myself, for not having fire extinguishers there?||||reg||8
Z||000010||Oreif||02-20-2001||09:44 AM||||Sorry to hear about your loss. What kind of home owners insurance do you have? Most home owners insurance cover vehicles stored in the garage. My car while in storage in my garage, is covered for damage/fire by the home policy and theft on the auto policy.<BR>Just something to check into.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||151 in. of love||02-20-2001||09:56 AM||||My agent said that nothing motor driven that was not intended for the use of home care would be covered. <P>I thought that sounded a little bit weird though. I mean that's why one receives and thus pays for insurance. To INSURE that if something happens, it will be replaced. Maybe I will probe a little deeper to find if he indeed told me the truth. <BR>  <BR>Anyone have any ideas as how to approach such a situation?||||reg||1
Z||000012||TheFiero64||02-20-2001||10:01 AM||||Sorry to hear what happened.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> I had a torch valve leak and go up in flames. I Luckily had a fire extinguisher near by. Very scary.<P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>88 Coupe V6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||1
Z||000013||bHooper||02-20-2001||10:21 AM||||dude, i would be almost cerain tha your homeowners would cover <B>anything</B> that is inside your home!  i had a leather coat stolen, at a bar, several new years ago... it was covered under homeowners.<P>if the car is in the garage,  it is in the home!||||reg||1
Z||000014||Oreif||02-20-2001||10:47 AM||||The vehicles were within the confines of the home. (within the walls) the Vehicles did not start the fire. The house damaged the vehicles. I would request a detailed report of what is covered under your home owners insurance. I know people who had a car on the driveway and the garage caught on fire. Pieces of the garage fell onto the hood and the home insurance covered it because the house damaged the car. That is like saying a guys car catches fire in a parking lot and your car is next to it gets damaged. Who's insurance covers it? His, because the damage came from it. If part of your gutter fell onto someones car in the driveway, your home insurance covers it. Get the report of exactly what is covered. I'm sure you'll find the clause/paragraph that mentions it.||||reg||1
Z||000015||stimpy||02-20-2001||10:49 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 151 in. of love:<BR><B>My agent said that nothing motor driven that was not intended for the use of home care would be covered. <P>Anyone have any ideas as how to approach such a situation?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Man, I sure am sorry to hear about your Honda generator and riding lawnmower, your Suzuki compressor, and your Yamaha leaf blower. Real damn shame abut your Pontiac vacuum cleaner, though.||||reg||13
Z||000016||Bald Dude||02-20-2001||10:50 AM||||Dude,  I know what it's like to lose a fiero in a fire.  I'm glad you are ok and you really should look into getting the fire dept to pony up a few buck!<P>Mark||||reg||1
Z||000017||Jaygee79||02-20-2001||11:14 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 151 in. of love:<BR><B>My agent said that nothing motor driven that was not intended for the use of home care would be covered. <P>I thought that sounded a little bit weird though. I mean that's why one receives and thus pays for insurance. To INSURE that if something happens, it will be replaced. Maybe I will probe a little deeper to find if he indeed told me the truth. <BR>  <BR>Anyone have any ideas as how to approach such a situation?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Wow, that's awful.  Get in touch with an insurance lawyer in your area.  They can tell you if you are covered for anything (which you should be, home owners insurance is supposed to cover ANYTHING inside of the house).||||reg||9
